                <h1>Cross-border E-commerce Paid Ads Budget Allocation SOP: From Account Setup to Weekly Optimization</h1>
<p>During the cold start phase of an independent website, the biggest risk isn't poor creatives—it's uncontrolled budget allocation. This SOP provides a battle-tested framework you can follow step by step.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Define Advertising Goals and Core KPIs</h2>
<p>Before allocating budget, the team must align on the following:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Question</th>
<th>Reference Standard</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>What is the core objective?</td>
<td>Customer acquisition / Cart abandonment recovery / Product testing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>What is the minimum acceptable ROAS?</td>
<td>Flash sales ≥2, Branding ≥3-5</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>What is the average order value?</td>
<td>Determines CPC and CPA thresholds</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>What is the daily budget ceiling?</td>
<td>Back-calculated from cash flow and expected LTV</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Pro tip</strong>: If the team lacks historical data, start with $500-$1,000/day for 3-5 days to establish baseline conversion metrics before scaling.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 2: Allocate Budget by Platform</h2>
<p>Each major traffic platform has distinct advantages. For cold start, use a "main + secondary + testing" three-layer structure:</p>
<h3>Primary Platforms (50-60% of total budget)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meta Ads (Facebook/Instagram)</strong>: Broad audience reach, ideal for product testing and retargeting, shorter creative lifecycle</li>
<li><strong>TikTok Ads</strong>: Best for visually striking products, lower CPM but requires conversion path optimization</li>
</ul>
<h3>Secondary Platforms (25-35% of total budget)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Google Ads</strong>: Clear search intent, suitable for products with specific category keywords, typically higher conversion rates</li>
<li><strong>Pinterest Ads</strong>: Ideal for home, fashion, DIY categories, relatively stable CPC</li>
</ul>
<h3>Testing Platforms (10-15% of total budget)</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Snapchat, Twitter/X</strong>: Selected testing based on target user persona</li>
<li><strong>Influencer collaborations/DM ads</strong>: Supplementary social traffic for independent websites</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 3: Budget Hierarchy Within Each Platform</h2>
<p>Using Meta Ads as an example, a healthy new account structure should include:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Campaign Type</th>
<th>% of Platform Budget</th>
<th>Typical Bidding Strategy</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Product Testing</td>
<td>40%</td>
<td>Lowest Cost</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Retargeting/DPA</td>
<td>30%</td>
<td>Lowest Cost ROAS</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Audience Expansion</td>
<td>20%</td>
<td>Manual Bidding</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Brand/Competitor Protection</td>
<td>10%</td>
<td>Cost Cap</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Rule of thumb</strong>: Each campaign should have a minimum daily budget of $50, otherwise the algorithm lacks sufficient learning data and may enter "account diagnostics" status.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 4: Adjust Budget Based on Testing and Scaling Phases</h2>
<h3>Testing Phase (Day 1-14)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Minimum $30-50/day per ad set</li>
<li>Test 5-8 creatives simultaneously</li>
<li>Decision criteria: Wait for at least 50 conversion events before judging performance</li>
</ul>
<h3>Scaling Phase (Day 15+)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Double budget for campaigns with positive ROAS</li>
<li>Maintain "testing campaigns" at 15% ratio</li>
<li>Re-balance budgets during weekly reviews</li>
</ul>
<h3>Explosive Growth Phase (ROAS ≥3 for 3+ consecutive days)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Immediately increase budget by 2-3x</li>
<li>Launch lookalike audience expansion simultaneously</li>
<li>Keep retargeting campaign budget unchanged</li>
</ul>
<h2>Step 5: Weekly Review and Budget Rebalancing</h2>
<p>Every weekend, complete a "budget efficiency review":</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Export campaign data</strong>: Focus on spend, ROAS, CTR, CPC, frequency</li>
<li><strong>Categorize campaigns</strong>: Star performers (ROAS 20%+ above target) → increase budget 30%; Maintainers (80-120% of target) → maintain or minor adjustments; Problem cases (ROAS below 50% for 3+ days) → pause or restructure</li>
<li><strong>Update audiences and creatives</strong>: Move validated testing creatives to scaling groups, continuously retire low-CTR creatives</li>
<li><strong>Document findings</strong>: Build a knowledge base for future reference</li>
</ol>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: How much should I initially deposit for a new account?</strong></p>
<p>Start with $1,000-2,000 to reserve 3-5 days of testing budget. Frequent deposits may trigger payment risk controls.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How to distribute budget when running multiple country markets simultaneously?</strong></p>
<p>Grade by market size and conversion cost: Primary markets (US/UK) 60%, Emerging markets (Germany/France) 25%, Testing markets 15%. Avoid targeting more than 3 core markets initially.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What if budget depletes too quickly?</strong></p>
<p>Check for audience overlap causing internal competition, and verify that spending isn't concentrated in peak hours. Distribute budget across different time periods to give the algorithm sufficient learning time.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Execution Checklist</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Confirm target ROAS and daily budget ceiling</li>
<li>[ ] Allocate "primary + secondary + testing" structure by platform</li>
<li>[ ] Layer campaigns within Meta by type</li>
<li>[ ] Maintain 5-8 creative tests during testing phase</li>
<li>[ ] Complete weekly "budget efficiency review"</li>
<li>[ ] Document data to shared records</li>
</ul>
<p>Following this SOP, teams can establish a stable budget framework for independent website advertising within 2-3 weeks and enter a sustainable optimization cycle.</p>
